  "articleSummary": "**Summary:**\n\nIn \"No Fate But What We Bill,\" the author delivers an analytical threat assessment targeted at engineering and security leadership, arguing that the true danger of agentic AI lies not in sci-fi breakthroughs, but in the mathematical multiplication of two compounding factors: autonomous task endurance and multi-agent orchestration breadth. The core thesis posits that defensive economic assumptions fail because attackers bypass traditional cost gates through \"LLMjacking\"—stealing cloud credentials to run massive, persistent swarms on the victim's dime. The analysis evaluates real-world technologies and benchmarks, specifically referencing METR’s task-horizon metrics, commercial coding agents (like Claude Code), and orchestrators coordinating dozens of sub-agents concurrently. Throughout the piece, the author uses a recurring *Terminator* metaphor—reframing the relentless killer robot as a cheap retry loop and Skynet as a distributed, metered API system—to illustrate an asymmetric war of attrition where defenders incur linear human costs while attackers scale autonomous attempts for free.",
